# Read entire files (mandatory — all tasks)

When the user **@mentions**, **pastes**, or **points to any file** in this project — read the **complete file** before changing code or giving implementation advice.

This applies to **every page and every file**, not only Dashboard:

- `resources/js/Pages/**` (Guidance, Evaluator, auth, etc.)
- `resources/js/Components/**`
- Controllers, models, migrations — when the task includes them
- Any path the user attaches or pastes in chat

## Required steps

1. **Read the full file** with the `Read` tool (line 1 through the last line). Do not rely on partial snippets, memory, or grep-only context for the file you will edit.
2. If the user sends **multiple files**, read **each file in full** before editing any of them.
3. **Follow imports**: when a page uses `Layout`, modals, hooks, or local components, read those files too if they affect behavior, props, or layout you are changing.
4. **Understand structure first**: props, state, handlers, routes, conditions, empty states, list keys — then implement.
5. **Do not skip** reading because the file is long; use offset/limit only to read in sequential chunks until the whole file is covered.

## What “understand the structure” means

Before editing, you should know:

- What the component exports and what props it receives
- Where data is displayed and which fields are used
- What user actions exist (clicks, forms, navigation)
- What must stay the same for the app to keep working

## Redesign vs other work

| Task type | Read full file | Backend edits |
|-----------|----------------|---------------|
| Redesign / reorganize UI | Yes | No (unless user asks) |
| Bug fix on a page | Yes | Only if needed and user agrees |
| New feature | Yes | Follow fullstack order |

## Do not

- Edit a file you have only partially read
- Assume Dashboard patterns apply to another page without reading that page
- Replace display logic without finding every use in the full file

User expectation: **“Read the entire code, understand the structure, then work.”**
